### What does the idempotent producer solve?

Without idempotence, a common duplicate scenario is: the producer sends a record, the broker writes it, but the ack is lost. The producer retries, and the broker now has the record twice. With `enable.idempotence=true`, the broker deduplicates using sequence numbers.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class IdempotentProducerConfig {
    public static Properties create() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");
        // Deduplicates client retries only -- not application duplicates

        System.out.println("Idempotent producer enabled");
        System.out.println("  Deduplicates: client retransmissions (same sequence number)");
        System.out.println("  Does NOT deduplicate: two separate send() calls");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        create();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Idempotent producer enabled
  Deduplicates: client retransmissions (same sequence number)
  Does NOT deduplicate: two separate send() calls
```

### What does it NOT protect against?

The sequence number is assigned per `send()` call. Two separate sends for the same business event get different sequence numbers. The broker stores both.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class ApplicationDuplicateDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            // Upstream HTTP request retried -- settle() runs twice
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("settlements", "SAV2001",
                "SETTLE TXF-123 50000")).get();
            System.out.println("First send: sequence N -- stored");

            // Same business event, NEW send() call -- different sequence
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("settlements", "SAV2001",
                "SETTLE TXF-123 50000")).get();
            System.out.println("Second send: sequence N+1 -- ALSO stored");
            System.out.println("  Two records on broker! Idempotent producer did NOT help.");
            System.out.println("  Priya gets double credit unless consumer deduplicates.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
First send: sequence N -- stored
Second send: sequence N+1 -- ALSO stored
  Two records on broker! Idempotent producer did NOT help.
  Priya gets double credit unless consumer deduplicates.
```

### How do you fix application-level duplicates?

Use an idempotency key with consumer-side deduplication. The consumer checks a processed-ids store before applying the effect.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class ConsumerSideDedup {
    // Simulates a database table with unique constraint on transfer_id
    private static final Set<String> processedTransfers = new HashSet<>();

    public static void applySettlement(String transferId, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (processedTransfers.contains(transferId)) {
            System.out.println("SKIP: " + transferId + " already processed -- no double credit");
            return;
        }
        // Apply in same DB transaction: credit + insert processed id
        processedTransfers.add(transferId);
        System.out.println("APPLY: " + transferId + " credit " + amount + " to " + accountId);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        applySettlement("TXF-123", "SAV3001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        applySettlement("TXF-123", "SAV3001", new BigDecimal("50000")); // Duplicate
        applySettlement("TXF-124", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("25000")); // Different transfer
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
APPLY: TXF-123 credit 50000 to SAV3001
SKIP: TXF-123 already processed -- no double credit
APPLY: TXF-124 credit 25000 to SAV2001
```

### How does the layered dedup model work?

| Layer | Mechanism | Protects Against |
|---|---|---|
| Idempotent producer | Sequence numbers per send | Client retry duplicates |
| Kafka transactions | Atomic commit of offsets + output | Within-Kafka exactly-once |
| Application idempotency key | Business id + dedup store | Application-level duplicates |
| Transactional outbox | Single DB transaction | Cross-system exactly-once |

### What about the transactional outbox pattern?

When a publish must be exactly-once together with a database write, use the transactional outbox so there is a single source of truth for whether the event exists.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OutboxPattern {
    // @Transactional -- one database transaction
    public static void transferWithOutbox(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // debit(from, amount);
        // credit(to, amount);
        // outboxRepo.save(new OutboxEvent("TXF-125", "TransferCompleted", payload));
        System.out.println("DB transaction: debit + credit + outbox row");
        System.out.println("  No Kafka call here -- relay publishes from outbox");
        System.out.println("  Single source of truth: the database");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        transferWithOutbox("SAV2001", "SAV3001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
DB transaction: debit + credit + outbox row
  No Kafka call here -- relay publishes from outbox
  Single source of truth: the database
```

### What about producer restart and session scope?

Idempotence is scoped to a single producer session. If the producer restarts and gets a new producer id, sequence numbers reset and the broker cannot correlate. This is another reason it cannot be your business dedup mechanism.

### What are common mistakes when relying on idempotent producers?

The most common mistake is equating enable.idempotence=true with end-to-end exactly-once. Idempotence only deduplicates broker-level retries within one session. If your application logic resends a message with a new sequence number, the broker treats it as a new record. Another mistake is ignoring external side effects: even if the broker deduplicates the Kafka write, an HTTP call or database insert triggered during processing still executes twice.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class IdempotenceScopeMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: assume idempotence = end-to-end exactly-once");
        System.out.println("  enable.idempotence=true does NOT cover:");
        System.out.println("    - App-level resends (new sequence number)");
        System.out.println("    - External calls (HTTP, DB insert)");
        System.out.println("    - Cross-session duplicates (PID changes on restart)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: layer defenses");
        System.out.println("  Broker side: idempotent producer (retry dedup)");
        System.out.println("  App side: transactional outbox (app-level dedup)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer side: idempotent handler (business-key dedup)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: assume idempotence = end-to-end exactly-once
  enable.idempotence=true does NOT cover:
    - App-level resends (new sequence number)
    - External calls (HTTP, DB insert)
    - Cross-session duplicates (PID changes on restart)

CORRECT: layer defenses
  Broker side: idempotent producer (retry dedup)
  App side: transactional outbox (app-level dedup)
  Consumer side: idempotent handler (business-key dedup)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Kafka transactions build on idempotence for within-Kafka exactly-once, but even transactions do not know that two sends represent the same business event. The most reliable dedup is end-to-end, on the consumer side, keyed by the business id. The idempotent producer is necessary but never sufficient for real-world duplicate prevention.
